---
project_name: SRE Terminal (Claude + AWS + Octopus + Kubernetes)
version: 1.0.0
status: design-phase
created: 2026-01-20
last_updated: 2026-01-20
author: Abby
organization: Filevine Inc.
---

PROJECT_OVERVIEW:
  name: Interactive Claude Terminal for SRE
  description: Multi-layer incident response tool that integrates Claude AI with AWS CloudWatch, Octopus Deploy, and Kubernetes for fast root cause analysis
  purpose: Dramatically reduce incident diagnosis time, correlate deployment changes with infrastructure issues, provide intelligent recommendations
  target_users: SRE team at Filevine
  phase_1_goal: Read-only observability layer - Claude can view infrastructure state but cannot execute changes

PROBLEM_STATEMENT:
  current_workflow: Manual checks across multiple systems during incidents (AWS metrics, logs, Octopus deployments, K8s pod status)
  pain_points[3]: Slow incident diagnosis (takes 10-15 mins manual investigation), Context switching between tools, Hard to correlate AWS changes with Octopus deployments with K8s pod failures
  time_to_solve: 10-15 minutes per incident
  frequency: ~4 incidents per month during on-call
  estimated_annual_time_cost: 8-10 hours of manual investigation
  cost_savings_potential: $150-200/month (SRE time saved) plus faster recovery

SOLUTION_ARCHITECTURE:
  core_concept: Claude API with read-only access to multiple AWS/infrastructure systems, runs in authenticated user's session
  authentication_flow: User runs `aws-vault exec filevine-prod -- sre-terminal`, session inherits AWS credentials and K8s context, all actions audited to CloudTrail
  data_sources[4]: AWS (CloudWatch, logs, EC2, RDS, Lambda), Octopus Deploy (deployments, releases, history), Kubernetes (pods, logs, events, resources), Ollama (local log parsing)
  ai_model: Claude Opus 4.5 (main intelligence), Ollama mistral (local parsing)
  key_feature: Multi-layer correlation - Claude sees infrastructure state, recent deployments, pod status simultaneously

TECHNICAL_DESIGN:
  language: Python 3.8+
  dependencies[8]: anthropic, boto3, requests, kubernetes, subprocess, json, os, asyncio
  entry_point: sre_terminal.py
  main_classes[5]: FullStackSRETerminal, ReadOnlyAWSGate, OctopusGate, KubernetesGate, HybridProcessor
  execution_model: Interactive REPL loop - user enters question, Claude queries systems, returns analysis
  session_management: In-memory conversation history, background monitoring for active incidents
  cost_model: Hybrid - Ollama for parsing (~$0), Claude for analysis (~$0.005-0.01 per incident)

PHASE_1_CAPABILITIES:
  name: Read-Only Observability Layer
  aws_read_operations[12]: cloudwatch:GetMetricStatistics, logs:FilterLogEvents, ec2:DescribeInstances, dynamodb:Query, dynamodb:Scan, rds:DescribeDBInstances, lambda:GetFunction, ssm:GetAutomationExecution, ssm:DescribeAutomationExecutions, ssm:GetParameter, iam:GetUser, iam:GetRole
  octopus_read_operations[6]: GET /api/deployments, GET /api/releases, GET /api/environments, GET /api/projects, GET /api/tasks, GET /api/deployments/{id}/progress
  kubernetes_read_operations[9]: get pods, get deployments, get services, describe pod, logs, events, top pods, get nodes, rollout history
  blocked_operations[15]: ssm:StartAutomationExecution, ec2:TerminateInstances, ec2:StartInstances, rds:ModifyDBInstance, dynamodb:PutItem, dynamodb:DeleteItem, s3:PutObject, lambda:InvokeFunction, kubectl scale, kubectl delete, kubectl apply, kubectl patch, rollout undo, POST /api/deployments, POST /api/releases

PHASE_1_WORKFLOW:
  step_1: User authenticates with AWS credentials via aws-vault
  step_2: Terminal launches with Claude + read-only access to AWS, Octopus, K8s
  step_3: User asks question about incident (natural language)
  step_4: Claude determines what data to query
  step_5: System fetches data from AWS/Octopus/K8s (read-only only)
  step_6: Claude analyzes correlated data
  step_7: Claude provides root cause analysis and recommendations
  step_8: User executes remediation manually in respective UIs (optional later: automated with confirmation)

FUTURE_PHASES:
  phase_2_name: Automated Execution with Safety Gates
  phase_2_features[4]: SSM automation execution with confirmation, Pod restart capability, Scaling actions, Rollback triggers
  phase_3_name: Proactive Monitoring
  phase_3_features[3]: Background incident detection, Alert correlation, Predictive recommendations

DESIGN_DECISIONS:
  decision_1: Read-only first, write later (safety)
  decision_2: User session inheritance (security - all actions audited as user)
  decision_3: Claude API vs local models (cost vs intelligence tradeoff)
  decision_4: Ollama for parsing + Claude for analysis (cost optimization - ~50% token reduction)
  decision_5: Multi-layer visibility (AWS + Octopus + K8s) vs single layer (better incident diagnosis)

SECURITY_CONSIDERATIONS:
  principle: Least privilege - Claude can only do what authenticated user can do
  session_model: Credentials passed via environment (AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_SESSION_TOKEN)
  audit_trail: All AWS calls logged to CloudTrail as user, Octopus calls in Octopus audit log
  mfa: Inherited from aws-vault session (already required)
  revocation: Session expires when user session expires (automatic)
  dangerous_operations: Explicitly blocked in read-only mode, can be enabled in Phase 2 with confirmation gates
  no_persistent_credentials: Bot has no persistent credentials, only user session

CLAUDE_CAPABILITIES:
  claude_tasks[7]: Correlate data across systems, Identify patterns in logs, Suggest root causes, Recommend actions, Format output, Explain findings, Ask clarifying questions
  context_size: Full conversation history maintained (incidents tracked across multiple messages)
  error_handling: Claude validates unsafe operations before attempting them, gracefully handles API errors, explains why operations are blocked

INFRASTRUCTURE_INTEGRATION:
  aws_auth: Uses existing aws-vault setup
  octopus_auth: API key in environment variable (OCTOPUS_API_KEY)
  kubernetes_auth: Uses existing kubectl config (from KUBECONFIG or ~/.kube/config)
  logging: Session logs to ~/.sre-terminal/sessions/YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM.log
  monitoring: Background task for incident correlation (optional, Phase 2)

EXAMPLE_WORKFLOWS:
  workflow_1_title: Queue Backing Up
  workflow_1_steps[6]: User asks "Queue on shard-f backed up", Claude queries AWS metrics, Claude queries Octopus recent deployments, Claude queries K8s pod status, Claude correlates: "Deploy v2.1.3 happened 30min ago, pod OOMKilled 25min ago, queue backed up 20min ago", Claude recommends rollback to v2.1.2
  workflow_2_title: Service Slow
  workflow_2_steps[5]: User asks "API service timeout rate 40%", Claude checks AWS RequestCount metric, Claude checks K8s pod resource usage, Claude finds pods out of memory, Claude checks pod logs "memory leak detected", Claude recommends scale up or rollback
  workflow_3_title: Pod Crashing
  workflow_3_steps[4]: User asks "Pod keep restarting", Claude queries K8s events (OOMKilled), Claude checks pod logs previous (shows memory leak), Claude checks Octopus (new deploy 1hr ago), Claude recommends investigate new version or scale up

PHASE_1_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN:
  step_1: Create project structure and Git repo
  step_2: Implement AWS gate (read-only CloudWatch + logs queries)
  step_3: Implement basic Claude terminal loop
  step_4: Test on real AWS data (shard-f metrics)
  step_5: Implement Octopus gate
  step_6: Test deployment correlation workflow
  step_7: Implement Kubernetes gate
  step_8: Test full-stack correlation
  step_9: Document usage and setup
  step_10: Create safety gates and validation

DELIVERABLES_PHASE_1:
  code: sre_terminal.py + gate modules + test suite
  docs: README.md, DESIGN.md, SETUP.md, USAGE.md, TROUBLESHOOTING.md
  tests: Unit tests for gates, integration tests with mock AWS/Octopus/K8s
  examples: Example incident investigations, transcript logs

ESTIMATED_EFFORT:
  phase_1_hours: 12-16 hours (1-2 days focused work)
  phase_1_breakdown: AWS gate (2hrs), Claude integration (2hrs), Octopus gate (2hrs), K8s gate (2hrs), Testing (2hrs), Documentation (2-4hrs)
  dependencies_setup: 30 mins
  total_time_to_first_working_version: 4-5 hours

COST_ANALYSIS:
  claude_api_costs_per_incident: $0.005-0.01 (with Ollama optimization)
  claude_api_costs_per_month: $0.20-0.40 (4 incidents)
  claude_api_costs_per_year: $2.40-4.80
  roi_breakeven: Saves 15 minutes per incident × 4 incidents = 1 hour/month, At $150/hr SRE time = $150 saved, API cost $0.40/month = immediate positive ROI

SUCCESS_METRICS_PHASE_1:
  metric_1: Time to root cause (target: 2-3 minutes vs current 10-15 minutes)
  metric_2: Accuracy of recommendations (target: 90% accuracy)
  metric_3: False positives (target: <10%)
  metric_4: User satisfaction (target: 8/10 ease of use)
  metric_5: API cost vs time saved ratio

RISKS_AND_MITIGATIONS:
  risk_1: Claude hallucinates recommendations (mitigation: Always require user confirmation before executing, add validation layer)
  risk_2: Credential exposure (mitigation: No persistent credentials, session-based only)
  risk_3: AWS rate limiting (mitigation: Cache results, implement backoff, use batch queries)
  risk_4: Kubernetes cluster access failures (mitigation: Graceful degradation, clear error messages)
  risk_5: Claude model drift (mitigation: Pin Claude version, test regularly, document expected behavior)

INTEGRATION_WITH_GTD:
  daily_log_entry: Log incident usage like "03:18 - Used SRE terminal to debug shard-f queue issue, Claude identified memory leak from deployment, time to resolution 8 minutes"
  rpg_character: Award XP for incident handling (50 XP base + 25 bonus for fast resolution + 10 for pattern identification)
  evening_review: Evening check-in captures incident lessons learned
  skills: Could become a GTD skill for faster access (optional enhancement)

DOCUMENTATION_STRUCTURE:
  readme: Project overview, quickstart, example usage
  design: Full architecture, design decisions, data flow
  setup: Environment setup, credentials configuration, dependencies installation
  usage: How to run terminal, common commands, troubleshooting
  implementation: Detailed build guide for developers

REPOSITORY_LOCATION:
  path: ~/code/dotfiles/projects/sre-terminal/
  structure: README.md, DESIGN.md, IMPLEMENTATION.md, src/, tests/, docs/, requirements.txt, .env.example

NEXT_STEPS:
  immediate: Export this design to VERSION_CONTROL, create detailed implementation guide
  week_1: Build Phase 1 PoC (AWS + Claude terminal)
  week_2: Add Octopus integration and test correlation
  week_3: Add Kubernetes integration and test full-stack
  week_4: Documentation, testing, Phase 1 launch

CONTEXT_FOR_NEXT_DEVELOPER:
  notes: This project was designed after handling real incidents today (shard-f queue backing up, DLQ investigation). The motivation is to reduce incident diagnosis time from 10-15 minutes to 2-3 minutes by giving Claude full observability of infrastructure state simultaneously.
  current_status: Design complete, ready for implementation
  key_insight: The power is in multi-layer correlation - Claude seeing AWS + Octopus + K8s together can identify root causes much faster than manual checking
  safety_first: Phase 1 is read-only deliberately - build confidence before adding execution capabilities
  ideal_time_to_build: When on-call shift ends or during low-incident period
  blockers: None - all infrastructure already exists (AWS, Octopus, K8s, Claude API access)
